=== HINT 1: Read child address from pipe ===
read(pipefd[0], &child_addr, sizeof(child_addr));
close(pipefd[0]);

=== HINT 2: Attach with ptrace ===
ptrace(PTRACE_ATTACH, child, NULL, NULL);
waitpid(child, NULL, 0);

=== HINT 3: Open /proc/pid/mem ===
char path[64];
snprintf(path, sizeof(path), "/proc/%d/mem", child);
int fd = open(path, O_RDWR);

=== HINT 4: Seek + write ===
lseek64(fd, (off64_t)child_addr, SEEK_SET);
int new_val = 42;
write(fd, &new_val, sizeof(new_val));

=== HINT 5: Detach ===
ptrace(PTRACE_DETACH, child, NULL, NULL);
close(fd);
waitpid(child, &status, 0);

=== KEY INSIGHT ===
/proc/pid/mem write requires: ptrace permission + PTRACE_ATTACH first.
Since Linux 3.12 the ptrace attach is mandatory even if you have permission.
ptrace_scope=0 (Yama): any process can ptrace any other with same UID.
ptrace_scope=1 (default): only parent can ptrace child.
ptrace_scope=2: only processes with CAP_SYS_PTRACE.
ptrace_scope=3: disabled entirely (locked - survives reboots on some distros).
Alternative (no ptrace): process_vm_writev() syscall - direct cross-process write
  without attaching, but also requires ptrace permission.
